# Target C: collar good-end train charge (corrected)

**Scope audit, 2026-10-04:** the endpoint specialization with
`x_B=O(sqrt(D))`, `t_D<=1.860+o(1)` has no unbounded canonical Type-II
survivor. Bad outer train ends have the sharper exact capacity `x_B+m_B`.
The weaker conditional bounds below remain valid. See the
[exact hull-side capacity audit](../docs/reports/target_c_hull_side_exact_train_capacity_audit_2026-10-04.md).

Date: 2026-10-03
Base main: fb0760ec6cf56d8f01a570a402a1985aa21a4dfe
Status: proof-only candidate, noncanonical. C-II and Level I remain open.

Let S denote the strict boundary-rhombus count from
research/target_c_high_t_boundary_rhombus_floor_2026-10-02.md.
Let T_boundary be the set of complete stopped trains containing at least one selected
outer rhombus side as a terminal occurrence.

Each selected outer side is a direct unit contact between consecutive force-hull
vertices, so it is a good convex-hull train endpoint.

A stopped train containing two selected collar sides has two good hull-side endpoints.
There are at most two such trains: two distinct trains with two good hull ends have
alternating boundary endpoint pairs, so their embedded train tracks cross; three such
trains would form a triangle in the triangle-free train-intersection graph.

If d <= 2 collar trains contain two selected incidences, then

    S = 2 d + (|T_boundary| - d),

hence

    |T_boundary| >= S - 2.                                  (1)

This improves the previous S/2 bound to S - O(1).

Among the collar trains, at most two can have a second good hull-side endpoint. Thus
at least

    |T_boundary| - 2 >= S - 4

other terminal incidences lie either in bad outer-walk intervals or on bounded
exceptional faces. The exact outer-walk partition has at most 2 x_B terminal edge
occurrences inside bad intervals. Therefore, if E_exc_boundary counts
exceptional-face terminal incidences belonging to collar trains,

    E_exc_boundary >= S - 2 x_B - 4.                        (2)

The total exceptional-face train-end capacity is Q + 4 f, so

    Q + 4 f >= S - 2 x_B - 4.                              (3)

Using the finite collar theorem

    S >= 4 per(F) - 6 t_D D - 6 nu - 9 x_B - 8,

gives

    Q + 4 f >= 4 per(F) - 6 t_D D - 6 nu - 11 x_B - 12.   (4)

In the current Type-II square-root branch,
nu = O(sqrt(D)), x_B = O(sqrt(D)), and per(F)/D tends to pi. Therefore

    |T_boundary| >= (4 pi - 6 t_D - o(1)) D,
    E_exc_boundary >= (4 pi - 6 t_D - o(1)) D.             (5)

At t_D <= 1.860 + o(1), the coefficient is 1.4063706143...

This is not a closure theorem. The exceptional-face perimeter budget can still absorb
the incidences. The new information is a distinguished linear family of collar trains
routed from good hull sides to exceptional faces. The next step must use cyclic order
and train-direction labels of this incidence network to force a crossing/rank
obstruction or a source-preserving strict diameter decrease.

No numerical search, verifier execution, or CI result is used.
